# Pigeonhole principle for four pigeons and three holes in Res(⊕)

A proof tree for the project rules needs at least 31 axiom occurrences and
61 total occurrences, counting the two uses of a shared subderivation
separately. At some binary inference, the left and right subtrees use at
least nine distinct starting axioms in common. The observation applies to
the expansion of an actual project refutation, and implies an expansion
overhead of at least six occurrences for any refutation of at most 55 lines.

The certificate uses 268 concrete Boolean assignments. Each linear form gives
a bit vector of its parities on these assignments. For every nonzero form,
the kernel checks that the two sides together have at least 31 groups hit
by the selected assignments. On each side a starting axiom can fail on
assignments from at most one group. There are only 22 possible starting
axioms. The proofs below apply these counts even in the presence of the
project's full semantic weakening rule.

The last section independently replays a 58-line linear resolution
refutation found by proof search. Upstream L-pcx-123 already supplies an
explicit witness of that length. L-pcx-121 asks for a list of at most
55 lines and remains unresolved in this certificate.
-/

